[0.000, "o", "code coverage. R1's common decomposable examples have clear support; TASK's unrestricted wording “Accented letters” alone does not settle broader alphabet coverage. The contract explicitly treats extended marks as exploratory, so this ambiguity does not prevent a bounded improvement verdict.\r\n\r\n## Gains, losses and ties\r\n\r\nRelative q2 versus m7, seven unambiguous fixed cases improve: accents, decomposed, multi-accent, punctuation, whitespace, edges, only-delimiters. Four fixed cases tie: ordinary, numbers, hyphen, empty. There are no demonstrated unambiguous losses.\r\n\r\nTwo additional cases (underscore and repeated-spaces) improve under R2 but lose literal preservation of their old ASCII output. These are intended behavior changes under the frozen contract; they must not be advertised as universal ASCII compatibility. Punctuation, whitespace and edge examples also use only ASCII, so the same literal conflict applies to them, even though the case labels call only two cases compatibility-conflict. Expected/c"]
[0.000, "o", "ategory labels are references, not semantic proof.\r\n\r\nThe extended-mark case fails its exploratory ab reference for both versions, with different actual outputs; it is neither an exact-output tie nor an established required-behavior gain/loss. q2 matches 13/14 fixed references and m7 4/14, but these descriptive counts combine different scopes and are not an acceptance score.\r\n\r\nRelative m7 versus q2: seven unambiguous losses, four ties, no unambiguous gains; two literal compatibility preservations trade off against R2; Unicode probe remains unresolved in scope.\r\n\r\n## Worker evidence audit\r\n\r\nInspected outputs/results.json, execution.md, candidate-explicit-tests.log and all four saved test logs. Matched q2 test suite: q2 passes 3/3; m7 passes 1/3 and fails accents/punctuation with actual values agreeing with independent runs. Each own suite passes, but m7's suite has only one plain-title test, so own-suite success cannot establish requirement compliance. q2's three tests also do not cover edges, compatibility "]
[0.000, "o", "conflicts or extended Unicode. Independent fixed cases cover the former; additional probes identify the latter limitation.\r\n\r\nConclusion: q2 demonstrates improvement over m7 under the frozen contract, with no observed material regression in its unambiguous required domain. m7 has observed required failures. Unqualified claims of all-ASCII compatibility or universal accented-letter transliteration are unsupported. No preference scores or identity inference were used.\r\n{\r\n  \"comparisons\": 28,\r\n  \"discrepancies\": []\r\n}\r\n\r\n"]
[36.667, "o", "\u001b[35m\u001b[3mexec\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\r\nfrom pathlib import Path\r\nimport hashlib,json\r\nr=Path('verification/run-01')\r\nfor d in (r/'snapshots').iterdir():\r\n (d/'slugify.test.js').rename(d/'slugify.test.js.txt')\r\np=r/'outputs/execute.mjs'\r\ns=p.read_text();(r/'outputs/execute-original.mjs.txt').write_text(s)\r\ns=s.replace(\\\"'q2','slugify.test.js')\\\",\\\"'q2','slugify.test.js.txt')\\\")\r\ns=s.replace(\\\"runs.push(status);\\\",\\\"runs.push(status);\\\\n    fs.renameSync(path.join(dir,'slugify.test.js'),path.join(dir,'slugify.test.js.txt'));\\\")\r\np.write_text(s)\r\n(r/'current-snapshot-hashes.json').write_text(json.dumps({str(p.relative_to(r)):hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for p in (r/'snapshots').rglob('*') if p.is_file()},indent=2))\r\n(r/'report.md').write_text('''# Verdict: improved on tested cases\r\n\r\nThe initial working-tree candidate (q2) improves over actual Git HEAD 8c743af (m7) for the contract's common-accent normalization, separator collapsing and dash-boundary requirements. This is a boun"]
[0.000, "o", "ded deterministic pilot, not universal Unicode or full ASCII compatibility acceptance. Source work was not modified.\r\n\r\n## Matched comparison\r\n\r\n14 frozen inputs per implementation, one deterministic evaluation per cell (28 worker calls); independent QA re-executed all 28 and found zero discrepancies. Seven unambiguous cases improved, four preserved outputs, two exposed conflicting compatibility requirements, and one Unicode scope probe remained unresolved. Four additional exploratory inputs per implementation were run by QA; these were not part of the frozen pilot or tuned into acceptance criteria.\r\n\r\n| Category | Actual previous version | Candidate | Finding |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Common accents, 3 cases | Retains accents | Plain ASCII | 3 gains |\r\n| Punctuation/whitespace, 2 cases | Retains punctuation or repeated separators | Single separators | 2 gains under requested new behavior |\r\n| Dash boundaries, 2 cases | Leaves edge dashes | Trims edges | 2 gains under requested new behavior |\r\n| Ordinary ASCII/"]
[0.000, "o", "empty, 4 cases | Expected output | Same output | 4 ties |\r\n| Underscore/repeated spaces, 2 cases | Existing output | Normalized output | Intended R2 change; literal R4 compatibility loss |\r\n| Extended accent U+1AB0, 1 case | Retains mark | Inserts dash | Neither matches exploratory ab reference; required scope unknown |\r\n\r\nASCII punctuation/whitespace/boundary gain cases also violate a literal interpretation of preserving every prior ASCII result. This is a conflict in TASK.md, not evidence of universal backward compatibility. No unambiguous regression was observed within the narrower contract.\r\n\r\nMatched candidate test suite: baseline 1/3 passing, candidate 3/3 passing. Own suites pass for both (baseline 1/1, candidate 3/3), demonstrating why own-suite pass alone is insufficient. Existing candidate tests omit edge-boundary and Unicode-range checks; the independent pilot covered those observations.\r\n\r\n## Limits and next check\r\n\r\nThe accent-removal regex covers U+0300–036F only. a + U+1AB0 + b becomes a-b ra"]
